<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there Atomholic. Let's take a look at your concept.</p>
<p>While I applaud your enthusiasm for mythology and desire to bring them to life, there are much better ways to do it besides simply retelling the entire myth word for word. That's what your Central Narrative reads like at the moment: a list of multiple anomalies that happen one after another without additional context or explanation as to their significance.</p>
<p>This does not make for an interesting narrative. Consider <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-1826">SCP-1826</a>, which also involves elements of the mythic Wild Hunt. Notice how they do not simply list the Hunt as a singular event, but weave it into a modern setting, where the Foundation and other characters are also intrinsically involved. You might need to do that here as well. Don't just re-tell a myth as an scp. Instead, find a unique angle / perspective from which to convey elements of the myth.</p>
<p>If you decide to revise the concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I'll take another look. Best of luck for now!</p>

